{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Refined Prototype and Capstone Defense","title":"Module 6 Completion Studio","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting module 6 completion studio to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense","ail606_supplement_module_6_completion_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"08","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/06/07"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_6_8_module_6_completion_studio_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_6_8_module_6_completion_studio_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_6_8_module_6_completion_studio_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_6_8_module_6_completion_studio_calibration"]},"content":"# Module 6 Completion Studio\n\n![Module 6 Completion Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/06-08-module-6-completion-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA checklist that says \"all six files submitted\" confirms assembly, not completion. The six capstone components, the iteration log, the AI-USE.md, the quality review, the theoretical defense, the peer responses, and the slides, were each produced at a different time for a different purpose, and nothing guarantees they tell the same story. A completion studio is the final pass that treats the package as one argument and checks it for internal coherence: do the claims in your slides match the data in your log, does your theoretical defense reference fixes your quality review demanded, does your AI-USE.md agree with the rationale in your matrix. Contradictions across components are the single most common thing a committee catches, because each document was checked alone and never against the others.\n\nThe deeper purpose is synthesis. A capstone is not six deliverables; it is evidence of a designer's growth, and the completion studio is where you make that growth legible, naming how your judgment changed from the first iteration entry to the last. That reflective synthesis is what turns a folder of files into a defensible body of work.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student submits and writes: \"Iteration log, AI-USE.md, quality review, theoretical defense, peer responses, slides. All six files complete.\" Every file exists, so the student believes the capstone is done. But the slides claim \"five rounds of learner testing\" while the iteration log records two tryouts, and the theoretical defense praises the segmenting on screen 3 while the quality review flagged screen 3's missing captions as a blocker. Each file was internally fine; together they contradict, and a committee that reads them side by side will find the seams the student never checked.\n\nThe completion studio catches this before the committee does. It reads the package as one document, reconciles the numbers and claims that disagree, and adds a synthesis of how the work changed, so the capstone reads as coherent evidence rather than six independently produced files stapled together.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe completion studio performs two operations that a submission checklist cannot: **cross-artifact coherence checking** and **reflective synthesis**.\n\nCoherence checking treats every quantitative and qualitative claim as something that must agree with the same claim wherever else it appears. The method is a reconciliation pass: for each component, list its load-bearing claims, then find every other component that touches the same claim and check for agreement.\n\n| Claim source | Claim to verify | Cross-check against | Resolution if they disagree |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slides | Testing counts, completion rates | Iteration log | Correct the slide to match the recorded data |\n| Theoretical defense | A principle applied to a screen | Quality review of that screen | Reconcile so the defense and the review tell one story |\n| AI-USE.md | What a tool produced and you kept | Iteration log rationale | Confirm the same decision appears in both, or fix the gap |\n\nA reconciliation pass means knowing which component to cross-check each claim against. Match each claim source to the component that can confirm or contradict it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Slides: testing counts and completion rates\",\"definition\":\"Cross-check against the iteration log; correct the slide to match the recorded data\"},{\"term\":\"Theoretical defense: a principle applied to a screen\",\"definition\":\"Cross-check against the quality review of that screen; reconcile so they tell one story\"},{\"term\":\"AI-USE.md: what a tool produced and you kept\",\"definition\":\"Cross-check against the iteration log rationale; confirm the same decision appears in both\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThe principle is that completion means coherence, not assembly. A package where the slides and the log disagree on how many tryouts you ran is not complete at any number of files, because the contradiction is itself a defect the committee will treat as carelessness or, worse, as inflation.\n\nReflective synthesis is the second operation and the one that distinguishes a capstone from a course assignment. It is a metacognitive account of how your design judgment changed across the module: what you documented in the first iteration entry (often raw version counts) versus the last (problem-change-evidence chains with rejected alternatives), and what you would do differently next time (log AI use in real time rather than reconstruct it). This is the demonstration that you internalized the practice, not just performed it, which is the actual outcome a capstone certifies.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is one capstone-package consistency check written two ways. Read the before version, then the annotated after version.\n\n**Before (assembly only):**\n\n> Submitted: iteration log, AI-USE.md, quality review, theoretical defense, peer responses, slides. All six files complete.\n\nThat confirms the files exist but never checks whether they agree. Now the same package run through a cross-artifact coherence check:\n\n**After (annotated coherence check):**\n\n| Claim source | Claim | Cross-check | Resolution |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slides | \"Five rounds of learner testing\" | Iteration log records 2 tryouts | Corrected slides to \"2 tryouts\" to match the log |\n| Theoretical defense | \"Segmenting reduced load on screen 3\" | Quality review scored screen 3 captions as a blocker | Added the caption fix to the log so the defense and review agree |\n| AI-USE.md | \"AI drafted Module 3 objectives, 2 of 5 kept\" | Iteration log rationale cites the same 2 objectives | Consistent, no change |\n\n**Synthesis beat (what changed across the module):** Early on I documented version counts; by Module 6 I documented problem-change-evidence chains and learned to keep rejected alternatives. Next time I would log AI use in real time rather than reconstruct it.\n\nThe after version treats completion as coherence, not assembly. It reconciles contradictions across components and adds a synthesis of how the designer's judgment changed, which is what makes the package defensible rather than merely complete.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run the reconciliation pass on the contradiction your slides hide\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Your slides say \\\"five rounds of learner testing\\\" but the iteration log records 2 tryouts. What is the correct resolution?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Leave both; each file was internally fine\",\"Change the log to say five rounds to match the slides\",\"Correct the slides to \\\"2 tryouts\\\" to match the recorded data\",\"Delete the slide claim entirely\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Reconcile toward the recorded evidence: the log documents what actually happened, so the slide is the inaccurate component. Inflating the log to match the slide would be fabrication, not reconciliation.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Your theoretical defense praises the segmenting on screen 3, but your quality review flagged screen 3 captions as a blocker. How do you reconcile?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Drop the screen 3 defense so nothing conflicts\",\"Add the caption fix to the log so the defense and review tell one story\",\"Remove the caption finding from the quality review\",\"Leave them; they discuss different things\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Both touch screen 3, so they must agree. Logging the caption fix makes the defense (segmenting helped) and the review (captions were a blocker, now fixed) consistent: one story about one screen.\"},{\"prompt\":\"With the numbers and claims reconciled, what does the synthesis beat add that the coherence pass cannot?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A longer file list\",\"A metacognitive account of how your documentation moved from version counts to evidence chains\",\"A second copy of the quality review\",\"More polish on the slides\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Coherence makes the files agree; synthesis makes your growth explicit. Naming how your judgment changed across the module is what turns a reconciled folder into evidence of a designer who developed.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you call the capstone done, run the package as one document, not six. Existence of files is the lowest bar; agreement among them is the real one.\n\n1. **Coherence test:** Does every number and claim that appears in more than one component agree across all of them?\n2. **Reconciliation test:** Where components disagreed, did you correct the inaccurate one rather than leave both standing?\n3. **Synthesis test:** Have you named how your design judgment changed across the module and what you would do differently?\n\nIf any test fails, the package is assembled but not complete. Reconcile the contradictions and write the synthesis before submitting.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student says: \\\"All six files are submitted and each one passed its own check, so the capstone is complete.\\\" Which distinction does this confuse?\" explanation=\"Each file being internally fine is assembly. Completion requires that claims appearing in more than one component agree across all of them. A package can be fully submitted and still indefensible if the slides and the log disagree on tryout counts.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It confuses submission (files exist and each is internally fine) with completion (files agree across components)\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Nothing; six passing files is completion\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It confuses coherence with reflective synthesis\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It confuses a blocker with a cosmetic flaw\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is equating a full file list with a finished capstone. Submission and completion are different states: a submitted package can contain contradictions that make it indefensible, while a complete package has been reconciled into one consistent account. The checklist confirms the first; only the coherence pass confirms the second.\n\nA second error is omitting the reflective synthesis because the artifacts \"speak for themselves.\" They do not speak to your growth; they show snapshots, and only the synthesis connects them into a trajectory. A committee reading a capstone wants to see that the designer changed, and the synthesis is the only component that makes that change explicit rather than leaving it to be inferred.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nLay all six components side by side and make a single list of every claim that appears in more than one of them: testing counts, completion numbers, which principle governs which screen, which AI output you kept. For each shared claim, check that the components agree, and when they do not, decide which one is correct and fix the others to match it. This is tedious and it is exactly where committees find the inflation and carelessness that weaken otherwise strong work.\n\nThen write the synthesis. Reread your first iteration entry and your last, and describe in plain language what changed in how you documented decisions, what you now know that you did not at the start, and what you would change in your process next time. The walkthrough ends with a package whose claims agree everywhere they appear and a synthesis that makes your growth as a designer the explicit conclusion of the capstone.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, pull the one number that appears in the most components of your own package and verify it agrees in all of them. Strong reading here means assuming a contradiction exists until you have checked, because the contradictions that sink capstones are precisely the ones the author was confident were not there.\n\nA strong reader of a completion studio can do four things:\n\n1. Treat the package as one argument and list every cross-component claim.\n2. Reconcile disagreements by correcting the inaccurate component.\n3. Distinguish submission (files exist) from completion (files agree).\n4. Write a reflective synthesis that makes design growth explicit.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong completed capstone is judged by whether a committee reading all six components finds one consistent story and a clear account of growth. That requires a coherence pass that reconciled every shared claim and a synthesis that names what changed in the designer's judgment. The standard fails when the package is merely assembled, because internal contradictions read as either carelessness or inflated claims, both of which a defense will expose. When you are unsure whether two components agree, annotate the shared claim and request a comparison between a reconciled package and an assembled one.\n\nA final paragraph for the artifact would read like this: \"I read the six components as one document, reconciled every claim that appeared in more than one, and corrected the slides to match the log where they disagreed. I closed with a synthesis of how my documentation moved from version counts to evidence chains, which is the growth this capstone is meant to demonstrate.\"\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_module_6_completion_studio\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the completion logic to a team deliverable, where the six components were authored by different people rather than one designer over time. The coherence problem intensifies: now contradictions arise not from your own forgetfulness but from teammates working with different numbers and assumptions, so the reconciliation pass becomes a cross-author audit before delivery. The reflective synthesis shifts too, from individual growth to a shared account of how the team's design decisions converged. The transferable skill is producing one coherent argument from independently authored parts, which is the condition almost every professional design deliverable, from an RFP response to a launch package, actually arrives in.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/06-08-module-6-completion-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"06.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 06.08. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_module_6_completion_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm every claim that appears in more than one component agrees, that you reconciled the ones that did not, and that you wrote a synthesis naming how your judgment changed. Completion is coherence, not assembly.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_8_module_6_completion_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_6_completion_studio","stem":"A student submits the Module 6 package as a zip of six finished files: iteration log, AI-USE.md, quality review, theoretical defense, peer-critique responses, and slides. What separates a defensible package from this assembly?","options":["The files should be PDFs, not a zip.","The components must tell one consistent story: the presentation's claims match the iteration log, the AI-USE.md, and the quality review.","There should be more than six files.","The files should be in alphabetical order."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Completion is coherence, not assembly: a defensible package is internally consistent across its components, not just a folder of finished artifacts.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_8_completion_as_assembly","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_6_completion_studio"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_8_module_6_completion_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_6_completion_studio","stem":"Your slides claim 'five rounds of learner testing,' but your iteration log records only two tryouts. What should you do before submitting the package?","options":["Leave both as is; reviewers rarely cross-check components.","Reconcile the components so the claim and the log agree, correcting whichever is inaccurate.","Delete the iteration log so only the slides remain.","Add three more testing rounds to the slides to round it up."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A committee will notice when the talk and the log disagree; cross-artifact claims must be reconciled before submission.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_8_cross_artifact_contradiction","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_6_completion_studio"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_8_module_6_completion_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI to write a one-page overview summarizing your whole capstone package. It states you 'conducted a formal usability study.' You ran informal tryouts. What is the correct boundary?","options":["Submit the overview as written; it summarizes nicely.","Verify every statement in the AI overview against the real components and correct any that the artifacts do not support before submitting.","Replace your tryouts with the AI's description so they match.","Use the AI overview only and discard the underlying artifacts."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An AI-assembled summary must be verified against each underlying artifact; submitting unverified claims you cannot defend undermines the package.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_8_ai_assembled_package_unverified","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_8_module_6_completion_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A reviewer says your completed package 'shows what you made but not what you learned.' Which addition best answers that?","options":["A cover page with a larger title.","A synthesis tracing how your design judgment changed across the module and what you would do differently next time.","A second copy of each deliverable.","A longer table of contents."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A synthesis of how judgment evolved across the module turns a list of deliverables into evidence of learning the reviewer is asking for.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_8_no_synthesis_of_learning","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"32472c11ec7cef87d7cb129c4a64da20e9e97b514149db6588eb8b96c6f0e997","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}